{:edb/db-before-t 1360 :edb/db-after-t 1361 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534673 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:53:23.456Z" 13194139534673 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302655 13194139534673 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302659 13194139534673 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302665 13194139534673 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302672 13194139534673 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186302662 13194139534673 true] [17592186064859 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534673 true] [17592186064859 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = t - 2t^{4}$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x = \\frac{1}{10}$ and velocity $v = \\frac{1}{10}$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$." 13194139534673 false] [17592186064859 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=t-2{t}^{4}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{10}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{10}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139534673 true] [17592186064859 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}{t}^{6}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}(1)^{6}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{15}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{10} & =\\frac{1}{10}+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{15}{t}^{6}.\n$$" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302655 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302656 13194139534673 true] [17592186302655 1089 "q-351551" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302655 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534673 true] [17592186302655 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=8{t}^{3}+12,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=10$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302655 1113 #uuid "43159458-17f0-5c7e-9f8b-8cea2a10b3e0" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302655 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (8{t}^{3}+12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+12t+C \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=10.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2(1)^{4}+12(1)+C \\\\ 10 & =2+12+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{4}+12t-4.\n$$" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302657 13194139534673 true] [17592186302656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302658 13194139534673 true] [17592186302656 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534673 true] [17592186302656 1041 "field-1" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302656 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302658 1319413953