n(t)+4" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534673 true] [17592186302661 1111 #uuid "ae61e0c7-5705-5152-8571-c717d9390da3" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302663 13194139534673 true] [17592186302662 1089 "q-351770" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534673 true] [17592186302662 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration \n\n$$\na(t)=\\frac{8}{(t+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534673 true] [17592186302662 1113 #uuid "abe8be45-2c0b-5637-9cac-f08714688de3" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302662 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{8}{(t+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\int (t+2)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{(t+2)^{-2}}{-2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(t+2)^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{4}{(0+2)^{2}}+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{4}{4}+C \\\\ 1 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by \n\n$$\nv(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+2)^{2}}+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{(t+2)^{2}}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4\\int (t+2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{(t+2)^{-1}}{-1}+2t+K \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+2}+2t+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{4}{0+2}+0+K \\\\ 0 & =2+K \\\\ K & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{4}{t+2}+2t-2.\n$$" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302664 13194139534673 true] [17592186302663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534673 true] [17592186302663 1041 "field-1" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186302664 13194139534673 true] [17592186302663 1110 #uuid "5c4b1fc2-5fe7-5d88-9fdb-9b9a0e91376e" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302664 1034 "2t+\\frac{4}{t+2}-2" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534673 true] [17592186302664 1111 #uuid "f73bf087-b07d-5a5e-89ac-8115d1ae3f2a" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186302666 13194139534673 true] [17592186302665 1089 "q-4783" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534673 true] [17592186302665 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}-2,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302665 1113 #uuid "fa2039cb-4bb3-5430-9351-2f1ef471a402" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302665 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =(0)^{3}-2(0)+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}-2t-1.\n$$" 13194139534673 true] [17592186302666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302667 13194139534673 true] [17592186302666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302668 13194139534673 true] [17592186302666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302669 13194139534673 true] [17592186302666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302670 13194139534673 true] [17592186302666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186302671 13194139534673 true] [17592186302666 1040 #edb/ref 175921860